Here is a simple Rust function that rotates the characters in a string by a given shift. The function uses the `chars()` method to iterate over the characters in the string, and the `collect::<String>()` method to create a new string with the rotated characters.

```rust
use serde_json::Value;

pub fn solve(input: Value) -> Value {
    let mut text = input.as_str().unwrap();
    let shift = input.as_i32().unwrap();

    text.chars().map(|c| {
        if c.is_lowercase() {
            let mut new_c = c as i32;
            new_c += shift;
            if new_c > 'z' as i32 {
                new_c -= 26;
            }
            if new_c < 'a' as i32 {
                new_c += 26;
            }
            c as char as_u8::from(new_c)
        } else {
            c
        }
    }).collect()
}
```

This function works by first getting the text and shift from the input JSON value. It then iterates over each character in the text. If the character is a lowercase letter, it adds the shift to the character and wraps around to the start of the alphabet if necessary. If the character is not a lowercase letter, it leaves it as is.

Please note that this function assumes that the input JSON value is a string and an integer. If the input JSON value is not a string or an integer, the function will panic.
